  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1028 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nOn Saturday, the Four Corners low will sweep eastward becoming \nlargely neutrally tilted as it passes 103W by dinner time whilst the \nCanadian portion of the feature moves from SK into MB and another\ndeeper system approaches the Pacific NW. Into Sunday, the Pac NW \nsystem camps out nearly stationary while we enter a period of \nzonal flow until a neutral trough, phased with another Canadian \nlow, transits early Tuesday morning. Thereafter, toward \nThanksgiving day, the solutions diverge though it would appear \nsome sort of system will influence our weather, but exactly what \nthe impact might be remains in flux.\n\nFrom a sensible weather perspective, the low tracking into the \nregion late Saturday will drive a cold front through on Saturday \nafternoon. Once the front encounters a bit deeper moisture along \nor east of our eastern column of counties (or further east yet), \nwe could see a few storms get going. At this point, with <500J/kg \nof CAPE and 0-6km bulk shear of 40+ knots, it appears a stretch \nthat many storms will become established let alone go severe. \nTemperatures, peaking on Saturday around 70, will be on a downward\ntrend for the rest of the week. Another low-end chance of \nprecipitation could exist for Tuesday into Wednesday though the \nlatest data keeps that activity to our north. That could change, \nbut the ECM-AI does tend to support the drier outlook whilst the \nGFS is slightly more favorable.",
